{
  "source": {
    "citation": "Cross-referenced from three outside-model research runs 2026-05-12 (LED candidates.md, Canonical LED Candidates.md, LED Characterization Data Request.md). Values reflect cross-source convergence with median or most-consistent value chosen where models disagreed; substantial disagreements noted per-instance.",
    "data_origin": "Published vendor datasheets. Some values estimated from luminous-efficacy-to-wall-plug-efficiency conversions (typically η_wpe = lm/W ÷ LER where LER ≈ 350-450 lm/W_optical for white LEDs depending on CCT/CRI).",
    "ingested_at": "2026-05-12",
    "ingestion_notes": "η_wpe (wall-plug efficiency) for white LEDs commonly conflated with luminous efficacy in datasheets. Real white-LED η_wpe at peak is typically 0.40-0.55 for premium parts at 2026 state-of-art; numbers >0.55 in source data typically reflect luminous-efficacy values not divided by LER. We use conservative median estimates here.",
    "pr001_parameter_ingest": "Ideality factor n, thermal coefficient dV_th/dT_j, and thermal time constant τ_th populated 2026-05-12 per PR-001 pre-registration requirements. Values are *literature-representative for substrate-class instance type* (chemistry + package class), NOT measured-per-specific-part. n from semilog I-V literature (Schubert 'Light-Emitting Diodes' 2nd ed; vendor characterization papers; SAE/IEEE proceedings). Thermal coefficient from InGaN/AlGaInP/AlGaN published averages. Thermal time constant from package R_th × C_th estimates. For F-003-led test, exact per-part measurement is required if higher precision than substrate-class-level is needed; literature representatives are sufficient for initial F-003-led protocol validation per PR-001."
  },
